@MH400 and nightraven:
You're both right. On one hand, competence isn't the sole requirement for modship, attitude is as well. Generally, users who obviously want the power for the sake of it (entirely or partly) are avoided, and if someone is asking for mod power, that's a good indication of this wrong attitude. That's why this rule is there. On the other hand, it's not as black and white as "guy asks for modship = no deal". You shouldn't interpret that rule by the letter.
@JazzDeath:
- There's no forcing C4C. If someone doesn't give good crits back, you'll know after a while, and stop giving him crits as well. There's nothing more to it than that;
- There's no need for you to deal with spam, flaming or excessive bumping any differently than other forums do;
- The "WIP in title" issue isn't really that big a problem for us to consider a new mod;
- The issue of copying someone's work is obviously a big problem, but if the rules are clear about it, one can simply report such a problem and move on.
@huevos:
Your attitude is completely wrong. There's no place for elitism in modding. "Raising the needs of the serious contributors above those of the casual mass" is out of the question. Leniency for a selection of users is unfair and wrong. None of that is happening, ever.
@DiminishedFifth:
In the C4C system, which is a system of reward, I don't think there's place for punishment. There's always going to be people who don't return the favour... Just ignore their next contribution and move on. It's a flaw that's inherent to the C4C system, and I don't believe
any
form of punishment will correct that flaw.
