The requests for such feature span back to at least 2010, and back then the answers were sounding like "we'll be working on it on the foreseeable future". I'm not sure what's exactly gone wrong in the ten years in between to prevent you from implementing such an apparently straightforward feature.
The official response on one of the threads I've linked was something like "we've realized that most of our users are bedroom players, not that interested to share their collection with other musicians" - and that's a very uninformed hypothesis that I'd seriously feel like challenging.
There's a possibility to create and share playlists now, but when you work with other musicians and you just want them to go through the songs you usually play you don't want to create a playlist that contains everything (and add the tabs to it one by one) and share it with them - oh, and remember to keep it up-to-date. The use case for sharing the whole list of favourite tabs is a real, plausible use case and I'm not sure why it's being ignored by UG staff.
Since UG won't seem to implement this feature in any way (sharing with other accounts through the app/site, generate a shareable link, export to CSV or JSON...), I've come up with my own JavaScript workaround, and I'd like to share it with anyone else who's interested in this feature. Pasting the following snippet in your browser's developers tools while you're on your fav tabs page will parse the table and return you a neat JSON object:
var artist = undefined;
[...document.querySelectorAll('._2vnXR')].map(item => {
let artistElement = item.querySelector('._18t7s._2KENO._2qUQQ');
let titleElement = item.querySelector('._18t7s._1EK1W._2qUQQ');
if (artistElement) {
artist = artistElement.innerText;
}
if (!artist || !titleElement) return;
return {
artist: artist,
title: titleElement.innerText,
link: titleElement.getAttribute('href'),
};
}).filter(item => item)
From there it should be fairly straightforward to export the list in any desired format (JSON, CSV...), as long as the designers don't decide to change the class names for the elements in the DOM (please, don't).
If I've managed to put together something like that in 10 minutes, I'm not sure what has prevent UG from providing such a feature for the past 10 years.

